1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,632
Let's build access policies with Gleam and E Y G.

2
00:00:03,632 --> 00:00:08,489
We will sign in, create commits, approve changes, and limit inference usage.

3
00:00:08,489 --> 00:00:13,729
For every allowed policy decision, the engine will now show its actual proof tree.

4
00:00:15,542 --> 00:00:17,348
First, write the E Y G rule.

5
00:00:17,348 --> 00:00:20,962
Join a user's channel membership with a role permission.

6
00:00:20,962 --> 00:00:25,608
Tenant, actor, action, and resource come from the authenticated request.

7
00:00:25,608 --> 00:00:28,124
A matching row is the permission proof.

8
00:00:29,958 --> 00:00:31,708
Now write the Gleam grants.

9
00:00:31,708 --> 00:00:38,384
Platform members may open the deployments page, change infrastructure files, and approve those changes.

10
00:00:38,384 --> 00:00:40,393
These are separate permissions.

11
00:00:40,393 --> 00:00:42,727
Unknown paths are denied by default.

12
00:00:44,542 --> 00:00:46,749
Ada chooses Sign in with Slack.

13
00:00:46,749 --> 00:00:49,171
Our mock completes the login flow.

14
00:00:49,171 --> 00:00:55,296
The Gleam host checks single use state, browser binding, nonce, workspace, and expiry.

15
00:00:55,296 --> 00:00:57,789
Ada then links her GitHub identity.

16
00:00:59,625 --> 00:01:03,740
Ada belongs to Platform, so deployments returns two hundred.

17
00:01:03,740 --> 00:01:05,797
Finance returns four oh three.

18
00:01:05,797 --> 00:01:09,295
These results come from the actual E Y G evaluator.

19
00:01:09,295 --> 00:01:13,136
The protected data loader runs only after authorization.

20
00:01:14,958 --> 00:01:18,483
Read the engine generated tree from the leaves upward.

21
00:01:18,483 --> 00:01:20,899
The Member fact puts Ada in Platform.

22
00:01:20,899 --> 00:01:24,555
The Role Permission fact lets Platform view deployments.

23
00:01:24,555 --> 00:01:28,080
Rule zero joins those exact facts and produces Permit.

24
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:31,474
Session checks are performed separately by the host.

25
00:01:33,292 --> 00:01:39,104
In an isolated local Git repository, Ada increases the worker replicas from two to three.

26
00:01:39,104 --> 00:01:42,239
We inspect the diff, stage the file, and commit.

27
00:01:42,239 --> 00:01:46,223
This is a real commit; the main project history is untouched.

28
00:01:48,042 --> 00:01:51,165
The mock pull request points to that exact commit.

29
00:01:51,165 --> 00:01:53,914
Ada has permission to change infrastructure.

30
00:01:53,914 --> 00:01:59,036
But there is no eligible approval yet, so the channel policy check blocks merging.

31
00:02:00,875 --> 00:02:02,962
Cal approves the pull request.

32
00:02:02,962 --> 00:02:05,536
Cal belongs to Finance, not Platform.

33
00:02:05,536 --> 00:02:10,545
The approval exists, but it cannot authorize this infrastructure change.

34
00:02:10,545 --> 00:02:12,215
The check stays blocked.

35
00:02:14,042 --> 00:02:16,821
Bea signs in and approves the current head.

36
00:02:16,821 --> 00:02:18,372
She belongs to Platform.

37
00:02:18,372 --> 00:02:23,866
Both submitter and reviewer permissions now have proofs, and the mock check succeeds.

38
00:02:23,866 --> 00:02:26,774
GitHub must require this check to enforce it.

39
00:02:28,583 --> 00:02:32,113
The passing pull request has two engine generated trees.

40
00:02:32,113 --> 00:02:35,580
Ada's membership joins the infrastructure change grant.

41
00:02:35,580 --> 00:02:39,172
Bea's membership joins the infrastructure approval grant.

42
00:02:39,172 --> 00:02:47,051
The host additionally verifies the linked GitHub identities, the current commit approval, and coverage of every changed path.

43
00:02:48,875 --> 00:02:52,908
Ada creates a second real commit, increasing replicas to four.

44
00:02:52,908 --> 00:02:55,770
Bea's earlier approval names the old commit.

45
00:02:55,770 --> 00:03:00,583
The current head has changed, so the policy blocks the pull request again.

46
00:03:02,417 --> 00:03:05,254
Bea reviews the new commit and approves again.

47
00:03:05,254 --> 00:03:10,066
The current head now has an eligible approval, so both permission checks pass.

48
00:03:11,875 --> 00:03:15,513
Here are the proof trees returned by the new evaluation.

49
00:03:15,513 --> 00:03:18,046
Both memberships and grants still hold.

50
00:03:18,046 --> 00:03:22,139
The host now associates Bea's fresh review with the new commit.

51
00:03:22,139 --> 00:03:27,791
The same policy facts explain access, while the host enforces commit specific approval.

52
00:03:29,625 --> 00:03:33,005
Now remove Bea from Platform and refresh membership.

53
00:03:33,005 --> 00:03:37,426
Reevaluating the same pull request removes her approval's authority.

54
00:03:37,426 --> 00:03:38,466
The check fails.

55
00:03:38,466 --> 00:03:43,277
Production webhooks or scheduled refreshes must trigger this reevaluation.

56
00:03:45,083 --> 00:03:47,097
Here is a second E Y G policy.

57
00:03:47,097 --> 00:03:53,474
Match trusted usage and quota facts for the same workspace, user, endpoint, and billing window.

58
00:03:53,474 --> 00:03:58,643
Only permit inference when usage is nonnegative and strictly below the limit.

59
00:04:00,458 --> 00:04:03,710
Ada has used forty nine of fifty inference requests.

60
00:04:03,710 --> 00:04:05,898
The policy allows the next request.

61
00:04:05,898 --> 00:04:11,275
Let's inspect the full engine generated proof before forwarding anything to the model.

62
00:04:13,083 --> 00:04:19,076
The quota is fifty and usage is forty nine, for the same user, endpoint, and billing window.

63
00:04:19,076 --> 00:04:25,981
Three predicates passed: the action is inference, usage is nonnegative, and forty nine is less than fifty.

64
00:04:25,981 --> 00:04:30,215
These are actual recorded guard results, with their bound values.

65
00:04:32,042 --> 00:04:35,670
Once the allowed request is reserved, usage reaches fifty.

66
00:04:35,670 --> 00:04:41,549
Fifty is not less than fifty, so the next call has no Permit row and no successful proof tree.

67
00:04:41,549 --> 00:04:45,740
The gateway must reserve usage atomically before calling the model.

68
00:04:47,542 --> 00:04:52,486
Proof trees now come from the Datalog engine, alongside its ordinary answers.

69
00:04:52,486 --> 00:04:54,990
Run the Gleam examples to inspect them.

70
00:04:54,990 --> 00:04:59,357
Slack and GitHub are still mocked, and the interface is illustrated.

71
00:04:59,357 --> 00:05:03,530
The video includes captured proof data and a readable transcript.
